'''''Cordyline congesta''''', often referred to as '''narrow-leaved palm lily''',<ref name=brain>{{ cite web |url=http://www.brisrain.webcentral.com.au/01_cms/details_pop.asp?ID=87 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060819083954/http://www.brisrain.webcentral.com.au/01_cms/details_pop.asp?ID=87 |url-status=dead |archive-date=19 August 2006 |title=''Cordyline congesta'' (DRACAENACEAE); Narrow-leaved palm lily |work=Brisbane Rainforest Action & Information Network (BRAIN) |year=2007 |access-date=30 July 2011 }}</ref> is an [[evergreen]] [[Australia]]n plant. It is a rare shrub that grows up to 3 meters (9 feet 10 inches) tall and was discovered on the margins of a [[rainforest]], and in riverine scrub and moist gullies in [[eucalyptus]] forest. Growing north from the [[Clarence River, New South Wales]].<ref>{{cite web |title=''Cordyline congesta''|work= PlantNET - NSW Flora Online|url=http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Cordyline~congesta|access-date=30 July 2011}}</ref>

Leaves long and thin to [[lanceolate]]. Up to {{convert|65|cm|ftin}} long by {{convert|4|cm|in}}, with stiff marginal teeth near the base of leaf; leaf stem up to {{convert|15|cm|in}} long. Small white to mauve flowers form on [[panicle]]s. Flowering occurs from September to October. Fruit an orange-red berry, {{convert|10|-|15|mm|in}} in diameter, ripening from December to March. This species propagates easily from seeds, suckers or stem cuttings.

Similar to ''Cordyline stricta'' and ''[[Cordyline rubra|C. rubra]]''; however, ''C.&nbsp;congesta'' has jagged, fringed or scalloped leaf margins, particularly near the leaf base.

